            <ArticleTitle>Preemptive levetiracetam decreases postoperative pain in rats.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>953-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Preemptive versus therapeutic effects of levetiracetam were investigated in a model of postoperative incisional pain in rats.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Levetiracetam (250, 500, and 1000 mg/kg intraperitoneal (i.p.) or morphine (5 mg/kg i.p.) was administered either 1 h before (preemptive administration) or 1 h after (therapeutic administration) incisional surgery to the hind paw of rats. The effects of levetiracetam were evaluated based on thermal hyperalgesia measured by the plantar test.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">All preoperatively treated levetiracetam groups showed a significant, dose dependent, increase in paw withdrawal latency. However, post-incisional administration of levetiracetam produced no antihyperalgesic effect at any dose or at any time. In contrast, post-incisional administration of morphine reduced thermal hyperalgesia, while preemptive administration of morphine did not produce any significant antihyperalgesic effects.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The present results suggest that levetiracetam might possess preemptive analgesic activity.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
